# ---- secrets -----------------------------------------------------------------
# eval/.env holds API keys. It is a template-tracked file (.env.example only).
.env
eval/.env
*.env
!.env.example
!eval/.env.example

# ---- derived Phase 3.3 artefacts ----------------------------------------------
# fix/rag/corpus/corpus.json is rebuilt byte-identically from the tracked
# raw_pages/ files by `python fix/rag/prepare_corpus.py`, so tracking it would
# only add 464 KB of duplication that can go stale.
fix/rag/corpus/

# RAG run outputs. Same reason as eval/results/ above, plus one more: these are
# currently mock-backend self-tests, and a tracked results_*.json is exactly the
# file someone later quotes as a result. The mock numbers live in
# fix/rag/RESULTS.md, labelled as mock, where they cannot be mistaken.
fix/rag/results_rag_*.json

# ---- eval run artefacts ------------------------------------------------------
# By default NOTHING under eval/results/ is tracked: raw model output and the
# intermediate stages are regenerated by re-running, they are large, and the
# Phase 2.4 checklist calls for a 50 MB budget, which does not belong in a
# repository.
#
# Four subtrees ARE tracked, because they are the evidence behind every number
# in the submission. A reader who cannot see them has nothing to check the
# claims against. Together they are ~657 KB, which is fine for git:
#
#   final_scores/  test_scores.json + statistical_report.md  (the numbers)
#   charts/        4 SVG figures + README.md                 (the figures)
#   narrative/     findings.md                               (the writeup)
#   test_run/      per-item rows: is_correct, parse_reason, raw_response
#                  (36 baseline / 36 improved / 73 values / 30 dialect)
#
# Two details of the syntax below are load-bearing:
#
#  1. `eval/results/*`, NOT `eval/results/`. A trailing slash excludes the
#     directory itself, and git cannot re-include anything beneath an excluded
#     directory - so that form keeps the four evidence directories ignored no
#     matter what the `!` lines say. `*` leaves the parent un-excluded, which
#     is what makes the negations legal.
#  2. These negations sit BELOW the `/results/` rule and below every other
#     pattern that could match them. Git is last-match-wins, so position is
#     the mechanism, not style.
eval/results/*
!eval/results/final_scores/
!eval/results/charts/
!eval/results/narrative/
!eval/results/test_run/

# Derived and per-request artefacts: full model output, the intermediate parse
# and score stages, and the run log stream. Regenerated by re-running; large.
eval/results/raw_responses/
eval/results/logs/
eval/results/parsed/
eval/results/scores/

# Dense-retrieval vector caches. Regenerated from the corpus; multi-MB binaries.
fix/rag/**/*.npz
*.npz

# A stray `results/` at the repo root (e.g. from a run invoked in the wrong
# cwd). ANCHORED with a leading slash on purpose: the unanchored form
# `results/` matches eval/results/ at every depth and, sitting further down the
# file, used to beat the four `!` negations above.
/results/

